{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0974"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge literature/language","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/literature__language.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",703],["+"," Even a fruit is claimed for Arabic: banana is often traced to Arabic banān, “finger” or “fingertip”, after the finger-like fruits of a bunch – although most dictionaries prefer a West African origin, Wolof banaana, carried into European languages by Portuguese traders in the 16th century."],["=",288],["+","\n| banana (disputed) | banān | finger, fingertip |"],["=",80]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",715],["+"," Sogar eine Frucht wird dem Arabischen zugeschrieben: Die Banane wird oft auf arabisch banān, „Finger“ oder „Fingerspitze“, zurückgeführt, nach den fingerförmigen Früchten einer Staude – die meisten Wörterbücher bevorzugen allerdings eine westafrikanische Herkunft, Wolof banaana, das portugiesische Händler im 16. Jahrhundert in die europäischen Sprachen trugen."],["=",286],["+","\n| Banane (umstritten) | banān | Finger, Fingerspitze |"],["=",82]]}]},
